# Who reviews what.
#
# The point of this file: each team owns its own content directory, so teams
# publish their own updates without waiting on a central reviewer. The last
# matching rule wins, so the catch-all goes first and the specific rules follow.
#
# For it to mean anything, protect the default branch in Settings → Branches
# with "Require a pull request before merging" and "Require review from Code
# Owners". Without that, this file is only a suggestion.
#
# Every rule below is commented out, and the handles are placeholders. A handle
# that does not exist in your organization is worse than no rule at all: GitHub
# cannot resolve it, so the review it was meant to require is never requested
# and the pull request looks approved-by-nobody instead of blocked. Uncomment as
# you go, and replace every handle with one of your own.
#
# See docs/adopting.md, section 3.

# The site itself — code, config, workflows, docs. Keep this with whoever
# maintains the fork, so a change to src/ needs them and a change to content/
# does not.
# *                                    @your-org/platform-maintainers

# Then two lines per team: its own definition, and its streamlines.
# /content/teams/devops.yaml           @your-org/devops
# /content/streamlines/devops/         @your-org/devops

# /content/teams/data-platform.yaml    @your-org/data-platform
# /content/streamlines/data-platform/  @your-org/data-platform

# Adding a team is two lines here and one file in content/teams/.
